Q1 = {
"Step 1 - Create an Account": "To open an account on Coinbase I need an email address, a strong password and a phone number that receives a verification code. After that the platform runs identity verification (KYC, \"know your customer\"). It asks for my full legal name, date of birth, home address, a photo of a government ID such as a passport or driving licence, and often a selfie to prove the ID is mine. It may also ask about my occupation and source of funds. Exchanges do this because criminals could otherwise use anonymous accounts to launder money, finance terrorism or dodge sanctions. Anti-money-laundering (AML) rules require it: in the USA the Bank Secrecy Act and the USA PATRIOT Act, in the EU the Anti-Money-Laundering Directives, and worldwide the FATF recommendations that most countries copy into their own laws.",
"Step 2 - Explore the Platform": "The dashboard shows my total balance, a price chart and a list of assets. The wallet section shows what I hold and lets me send or receive. The trading area lets me place orders. I looked at three coins. <b>Bitcoin (BTC)</b> is the first cryptocurrency and works as digital money and a store of value; it is different because its supply is capped at 21 million coins. <b>Ethereum (ETH)</b> is a blockchain that runs smart contracts and apps, and ETH pays the fees; it is different because it is programmable, which is what we use in Question 2. <b>USD Coin (USDC)</b> is a stablecoin designed to stay worth about one US dollar, used for payments and for moving money between exchanges; it is different because its price hardly moves while BTC and ETH swing a lot.",
"Step 3 - Add a Payment Method": "In the payment settings I choose \"Add a payment method\". For a bank account I give the bank name and my account and routing numbers (or log in to my bank through a secure link), and Coinbase may send two tiny test deposits to confirm it is mine. For a debit card I enter the card number, expiry date, CVV code and billing address. Comparing the two: a debit card is usually <b>faster</b>, because the purchase is often instant, but it normally has the <b>higher fee</b>. A bank transfer is <b>cheaper</b>, often with a much lower fee, but can take a few days to clear.",
"Step 4 - Simulate a Purchase": "I picked Ethereum and imagined spending $100. A <b>market order</b> buys immediately at the best price available now, so it is fast but the final price can move slightly. A <b>limit order</b> lets me set the price I am willing to pay and only fills if the market reaches it, so I control the price but the order may never fill. I chose a market order because I want to see how a simple purchase works and a small amount makes price movement unimportant. For an <i>example</i> only: if the fee were 1.5% ($1.50), $98.50 would be spent on ETH, and if ETH cost $3,000 I would receive about 0.0328 ETH. The real figures appear on the confirmation screen before I press Buy.",
"Step 5 - Review the Transaction Record": "The transaction history lists the type (buy, sell, send, receive), the asset, the amount, the date and time, the status, the fee and the payment method. For transfers on the blockchain it also shows a <b>transaction hash</b>, a long unique ID of that transaction. It matters because it is a receipt anyone can paste into a block explorer such as Etherscan to confirm that the transaction exists, see its status and see which addresses were involved.",
"Step 6 - Security Features": "<b>Two-factor authentication (2FA)</b> asks for a second proof, such as a code from an authenticator app or a hardware key, as well as my password. It matters because a stolen password alone cannot open my account. <b>Withdrawal limits and address allow-listing</b> cap how much can leave my account per day and can restrict sending to addresses I approved earlier. This matters because if a hacker did get in, they could not empty the account at once, and blockchain transfers cannot be reversed.",
}
